1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,883
In C, #include pastes a file into yours.

2
00:00:04,933 --> 00:00:10,390
In PHP, require does the same.
Python's import does not.

3
00:00:11,233 --> 00:00:15,717
Import tree_node, then use the
class, and you get a NameError.

4
00:00:15,767 --> 00:00:18,423
Import gave you one name: the module.

5
00:00:18,473 --> 00:00:20,543
The class is still inside it.

6
00:00:21,367 --> 00:00:25,275
So reach the class through
the module, with a dot.

7
00:00:25,325 --> 00:00:28,997
Or use from … import to
bring in that one name.

8
00:00:29,833 --> 00:00:30,877
Second surprise.

9
00:00:30,927 --> 00:00:36,274
This file imports its neighbour, in the
same folder, and Python cannot find it.

10
00:00:36,324 --> 00:00:39,075
Include looks next to the file that asks.

11
00:00:39,125 --> 00:00:42,423
Python looks in the folder
of the script you ran.

12
00:00:42,767 --> 00:00:47,517
So write the path from that
folder: utils.tree_node.

13
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:49,050
One more.

14
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:51,522
A file name is a module name.

15
00:00:51,572 --> 00:00:56,123
So no hyphens, and no leading
digits. Use underscores.

16
00:00:56,967 --> 00:00:57,142
So.

17
00:00:57,192 --> 00:01:00,454
Import gives you a
module, not its contents.

18
00:01:00,504 --> 00:01:03,163
Paths start from the script you ran.

19
00:01:03,213 --> 00:01:05,797
And name your files like variables.
